{
  "image_name": "0ee3f254059aafe6617099ce56b2f7e6.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how neuronal cargo transport changes as neurons mature from an unpolarized stage to a fully polarized cell with distinct axon and dendrites. Early on, transport appears relatively unrestricted: vesicles, organelles, and membrane proteins move through immature neurites without strong compartmental bias. As polarity develops, the axon begins to receive preferential cargo flow, including growth-related material, supported by adhesive cues such as integrins and L1CAM at the cell surface.\n\nWith maturation, trafficking becomes strongly selective. Presynaptic cargo is prioritized for delivery into the axon, where synaptic vesicle components support neurotransmitter release machinery, while growth-associated protein delivery declines. Mitochondria can be anchored locally, limiting their movement. A key transition is the formation of a selective filter near the axon initial segment or pre-axonal exclusion zone, which excludes non-axonal cargo and gates transport into the axon.\n\nThe bottom portion emphasizes the mature transport architecture: dendritic and axonal microtubules differ in polarity, and distinct motor proteins drive directional movement. Kinesin and dynein/dynactin mediate polarized anterograde and retrograde transport, while different endosome populations are sorted either to dendrites, axon, or excluded from inappropriate compartments.",
